WEBVTT

NOTE placeholder captions for V2 from the storyboard narration (temp)

V2-1-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.172
This is the HR case from Lesson 1.

V2-1-2
00:00:03.172 --> 00:00:05.948
A services company with about 400 employees.

V2-1-3
00:00:05.948 --> 00:00:13.879
HR receives about 90 requests a month, and most of them repeat: vacation days, pension terms, expense submissions, overtime policy.

V2-1-4
00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:19.431
They come in by WhatsApp, by email, and in person, with no organized system.

V2-1-5
00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:23.000
Nobody knows how many of them need human judgment.

V2-2-1
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.763
The tempting first move is a chatbot.

V2-2-2
00:00:25.763 --> 00:00:26.553
Not yet.

V2-2-3
00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:27.737
First we map.

V2-2-4
00:00:27.737 --> 00:00:34.842
And we map AS-IS: how it actually happens on a normal day, not how it's supposed to work.

V2-2-5
00:00:34.842 --> 00:00:36.421
No fixing, no AI.

V2-2-6
00:00:36.421 --> 00:00:38.000
Just what's really there.

V2-3-1
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.471
Field 1 is the process name.

V2-3-2
00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:45.000
Keep it plain and specific: answering employees' repeat questions to HR.

V2-3-3
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.529
Field 2 is the trigger, the thing that starts the process.

V2-3-4
00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:54.059
A good trigger is concrete: a message, a form, a date.

V2-3-5
00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:56.118
Something you can point to.

V2-3-6
00:00:56.118 --> 00:00:59.000
Before I fill it in, you choose.

V2-4-1
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.653
All three channels go on the map, because all three really happen.

V2-4-2
00:01:03.653 --> 00:01:06.755
Notice that nothing here is fixed or improved.

V2-4-3
00:01:06.755 --> 00:01:08.306
It's simply what happens.

V2-4-4
00:01:08.306 --> 00:01:12.959
Where a process starts and ends is a decision, not a given.

V2-4-5
00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:18.000
Without clear boundaries, a process swells until it includes almost the whole organization.

V2-5-1
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.068
Field 3 is the steps, each with a role owner.

V2-5-2
00:01:22.068 --> 00:01:24.915
Three to seven steps are usually enough.

V2-5-3
00:01:24.915 --> 00:01:26.542
This case needs three.

V2-5-4
00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:27.763
Receiving the request.

V2-5-5
00:01:27.763 --> 00:01:30.610
Checking the topic, done by HR staff.

V2-5-6
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:35.898
Then an answer or an escalation, by HR staff or the HR manager.

V2-5-7
00:01:35.898 --> 00:01:42.000
Owners are written as roles, not names, so the map stays true when people change.

V2-6-1
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.833
Look at the lanes building on screen.

V2-6-2
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:52.119
Each role gets its own row, so at every moment you can see who is holding the work.

V2-6-3
00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:56.571
Field 4 asks where responsibility passes from one role to another.

V2-6-4
00:01:56.571 --> 00:01:59.000
Look at who owns each step.

V2-7-1
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.521
From HR staff to the HR manager, when a question goes beyond known policy.

V2-7-2
00:02:04.521 --> 00:02:07.676
That's the most dangerous moment in a process.

V2-7-3
00:02:07.676 --> 00:02:13.197
The staffer assumes the manager knows what happened before, and that isn't always true.

V2-7-4
00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:21.479
A good map marks each handoff: from whom, to whom, what exactly passes, and what happens if the receiver is unavailable.

V2-7-5
00:02:21.479 --> 00:02:24.239
Here, there's no single channel for it.

V2-7-6
00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:27.000
Keep that in mind for Field 7.

V2-8-1
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.762
Field 5 is knowledge sources: the vacation, pension, expense, and overtime policies.

V2-8-2
00:02:31.762 --> 00:02:36.127
Each source gets an owner, the role that keeps it current.

V2-8-3
00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:37.714
Here, the HR manager.

V2-8-4
00:02:37.714 --> 00:02:43.270
And each gets a sensitivity tag on a four-level scale: public, internal, confidential, restricted.

V2-8-5
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.857
These policies are internal.

V2-8-6
00:02:44.857 --> 00:02:52.000
An employee's own personal or financial details are confidential, and they don't go into a public AI tool.

V2-9-1
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.174
Field 6 is existing controls.

V2-9-2
00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:58.087
Is anything checking the answers before they go out?

V2-9-3
00:02:58.087 --> 00:02:59.826
You've seen the steps.

V2-9-4
00:02:59.826 --> 00:03:02.000
What goes in the map?

V2-10-1
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.684
No formal control: it depends on the staffer's own judgment.

V2-10-2
00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:09.000
That's a valid answer, and exactly what mapping reveals.

V2-11-1
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.677
Field 7 is gaps.

V2-11-2
00:03:10.677 --> 00:03:14.032
Here you're allowed, even encouraged, to write unclear.

V2-11-3
00:03:14.032 --> 00:03:17.806
Nobody knows how many wrong answers were given before.

V2-11-4
00:03:17.806 --> 00:03:20.323
There's no single organized handoff channel.

V2-11-5
00:03:20.323 --> 00:03:22.000
That's a diagnostic result.

V2-12-1
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.318
Field 8 is the success metric.

V2-12-2
00:03:24.318 --> 00:03:29.341
Baseline: about 35 percent of the HR team's time goes to repeat questions.

V2-12-3
00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:31.659
Target: 20 percent within three months.

V2-12-4
00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:32.818
Not a feeling.

V2-12-5
00:03:32.818 --> 00:03:35.136
A number you can check later.

V2-12-6
00:03:35.136 --> 00:03:39.000
Next, you take the mapper's seat, starting with Field 5.
